I’ve been testing transactional state stores in my load-test cluster, and I noticed that there are store-wide write stalls triggered by the {{offsets}} column family:


{code:java}
[offsets] Stopping writes because we have 2 immutable memtables (waiting for flush), max_write_buffer_number is set to 2{code}
 
That’s from the Rocksdb logs, I see over 220 stalls caused by the {{offsets}} CF, and 0 stalls caused by the {{default}} CF.
While reading the code for the RocksdbStore I noticed that in RocksDBStore.openDB(), the offsets CF receives a new ColumnFamilyOptions instance. We don’t set a config for max_write_buffer_number, so it inherits RocksDB’s default of 2. Now that we have atomic flushes enabled for both default and offsets CF, any flush triggered by the default CF also flush the offsets CF’s memtable. If another flush is scheduled before the earlier flush completes, the offsets CF can accumulate two unflushed immutable memtables and stop writes to the entire RocksDB instance.

I believe that’s a regression because it limits users’ ability to increase memtable buffering to tolerate temporary memtable flush backlogs.

Paused tasks stay registered in StoreChangelogReader#changelogs, but restore() only receives the updating tasks. maybeUpdateLimitOffsetsForStandbyChangelogs() picks its partitions from all registered changelogs, including those of paused standby tasks:
{code:java}
final Set<TopicPartition> changelogsWithLimitOffsets = changelogs.entrySet().stream().filter(
    entry -> entry.getValue().stateManager.taskType() == Task.TaskType.STANDBY &&
    entry.getValue().stateManager.changelogAsSource(entry.getKey()))
  .map(Map.Entry::getKey).collect(Collectors.toSet());
{code}
If listConsumerGroupOffsets() fails with a retriable exception, committedOffsetForChangelogs() calls getTasksFromPartitions(). For a paused task, tasks.get() returns null, so null ends up in the task set, and maybeInitTaskTimeoutOrThrow() dereferences it:
{code:java}
tasks.forEach(t -> t.maybeInitTaskTimeoutOrThrow(now, cause));  {code}
clearTaskTimeout() guards against null, but maybeInitTaskTimeoutOrThrow() does not. The NullPointerException kills the state updater thread. 

changelogsWithLimitOffsets should only contain changelogs of tasks in tasks, the same check fetchableChangelogs() makes.

