h3. Problem

{{UnifiedLog}} registers its {{kafka.log:type=Log}} gauges ({{Size}}, {{NumLogSegments}}, {{LogStartOffset}}, {{LogEndOffset}}, {{RetentionSizeInPercent}}) in its constructor ({{UnifiedLog#newMetrics}}). {{LogManager#loadLog}} always constructs a {{UnifiedLog}} for every directory it finds, and then, for stray directories, neither tracks the log nor removes its metrics:

* a directory whose name ends with {{-stray}} (logs "Loaded stray log");
* a log for which {{isStray}} returns true (the directory is renamed to {{*-stray}}).

Since the log is not put into {{currentLogs}} / {{futureLogs}} / {{logsToBeDeleted}}, nothing calls {{UnifiedLog#removeLogMetrics}} for it later. The runtime path does clean up: {{asyncDelete}} goes through {{removeLogAndMetrics}}. Only the startup path leaks.

The gauges also keep the {{UnifiedLog}} object reachable for the lifetime of the JVM.

h3. Impact

The leaked gauges are tagged with the same {{topic}} and {{partition}} as a live replica would be. If the broker also hosts a live replica of the same partition (for example a stale directory left after a reassignment, next to the current replica), both logs register gauges with an identical name. Yammer's {{newGauge}} returns the gauge that is already registered for that name, so the series can be bound to the stray log instead of the live one. We saw {{Size}} reporting the size of the stray directory (about three times the size of the live replica) on 4.3.0, which makes partition size dashboards and alerts wrong.

h3. Steps to reproduce

Unit test against trunk (b2ab2389a8), added to {{LogManagerTest}}:

