A paused task keeps its changelogs registered and assigned to the restore consumer (DefaultStateUpdater#pauseTask). StoreChangelogReader pauses those partitions on the restore consumer, but the consumer can still raise an InvalidOffsetException for them:
 # A fetch that was in flight when the task was paused returns OFFSET_OUT_OF_RANGE. FetchCollector#collectFetch initializes the completed fetch before it checks isPaused().
 # Position validation after a leader change detects truncation. partitionsNeedingValidation() does not filter paused partitions.

StoreChangelogReader#pollRecordsFromRestoreConsumer turns this into a TaskCorruptedException that names the paused task. DefaultStateUpdater#handleTaskCorruptedException looks the task up only in updatingTasks:

 
{code:java}
final Task corruptedTask = updatingTasks.get(taskId);
if (corruptedTask == null) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Task " + taskId + " is corrupted but is not updating. " + BUG_ERROR_MESSAGE);
}
{code}
 

The IllegalStateException is fatal for the state updater thread, so every task fails. handleStreamsExceptionWithTask already handles a failed task that is in pausedTasks.

*Proposed fix:* handle a corrupted task in pausedTasks the same way as an updating one (mark its changelogs corrupted, checkpoint, unregister them), and remove it with addToExceptionsAndFailedTasksThenRemoveFromPausedTasks.

