*Description*

Consider a topology that joins A with B, joins C with D, and then joins the original streams B and C. All four repartition topics must have the same partition count to satisfy the three joins.

When the topology builder processes the final join, it extends only one of the two existing copartition groups. As a result, the Streams group coordinator can request different partition counts for the two inputs to a join. When the coordinator later sees topics with the counts it requested, it can reject the topology because those counts violate the join’s copartitioning requirement.

 

*Reproduction example*

 
{code:java}
StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();
var consumed = Consumed.with(Serdes.String(), Serdes.String());

var a = builder.stream("input-a", consumed).repartition(Repartitioned.as("a"));
var b = builder.stream("input-b", consumed).repartition(Repartitioned.as("b"));
var c = builder.stream("input-c", consumed).repartition(Repartitioned.as("c"));
var d = builder.stream("input-d", consumed).repartition(Repartitioned.as("d"));

var window = JoinWindows.ofTimeDifferenceWithNoGrace(Duration.ofMinutes(1));
ValueJoiner<String, String, String> combine = (left, right) -> left + ":" + right;

a.join(b, combine, window).to("out-ab");
c.join(d, combine, window).to("out-cd");
b.join(c, combine, window).to("out-bc");

Topology topology = builder.build();
{code}
 

builder.build() succeeded, but InternalTopologyBuilder.copartitionGroups() returned two overlapping groups, \{A, B} and \{B, C, D}. The coordinator kept A and B at one partition when it processed the first group. It then set B, C, and D to five partitions when it processed the second group. A remained at one partition, leaving the two inputs to a.join(b, ...) with different partition counts.

 

If A=1, B=1, C=1, D=5, repartition topic count will A=1, B=5, C=,5, D=5.

