h2. Problem

{{NamedCache#flush}} and {{NamedCache#clear}} reset the {{dirtyKeys}} {{LinkedHashSet}} with {{{}clear(){}}}. {{HashMap#clear()}} nulls every slot of its table, and the table never shrinks. Thus each reset costs O(peak dirty keys), not O(current dirty keys).

After one flush with many distinct dirty keys (up to the entry limit of the cache), every later flush of that cache pays for the large table, for the life of the task:
 * *CPU:* after a peak of 100k keys, a flush of 10 keys spends ~100–270 µs on {{{}clear(){}}}.
 * *Heap:* the table keeps ~5–11 bytes for each key of the peak. {{statestore.cache.max.bytes}} does not count it, and each store cache keeps its own peak table, so the total can be larger than the configured budget.

In a production profile of an application with a high cardinality of keys in multiple stores, we found a huge fraction of CPU time being spent in {{dirtyKeys.clear().}}
h2. Proposed change

Clear in place while the table matches the load. Replace the set when the load drops below a quarter of the peak:
{code:java}
private void resetDirtyKeys() {
    final int size = dirtyKeys.size();
    dirtyKeysPeak = Math.max(dirtyKeysPeak, size);
    if (size < dirtyKeysPeak / 4) {
        dirtyKeys = new LinkedHashSet<>();
        dirtyKeysPeak = 0;
    } else {
        dirtyKeys.clear();
    }
}
{code}
Call it from {{flush}} (also in the {{dirtyKeys.isEmpty()}} early return, so an idle store releases the table) and from {{{}clear{}}}. The reset cost then stays within a fixed multiple of the flush size.

A replacement on every flush ({{{}new LinkedHashSet<>(){}}}) is simpler, but at a high steady cardinality it allocates a humongous table on each flush (see below).
h2. Benchmark

{{NamedCacheDirtyKeysFlushBenchmark}} (JMH, included with the fix) models one put/flush cycle on random distinct keys. {{steady}} varies ±10%; {{burstN}} varies ±50% with a 0.1% chance of an N-key cycle. Mean µs for each cycle (JDK 17, G1, 2 GB heap):
||Workload||Keys||clear() (current)||clear-or-replace||always replace||
|steady|100|20.9|*19.3 (-8%)*|20.8 (-0%)|
|steady|1,000|217|*204 (-6%)*|236 (+9%)|
|steady|100,000|*43,677*|43,764 (+0%)|44,822 (+3%) *|
|burst10k|100|30.8|*24.3 (-21%)*|40.3 (+31%)|
|burst100k|10|288|*72 (-75%)*|88 (-69%)|
|burst100k|1,000|532|*303 (-43%)*|504 (-5%)|

* 17 GCs / 787 ms against 2 GCs / 5–7 ms for the other two strategies, and p99 +100%.

Across all 11 scenarios, clear-or-replace has the best mean in 9, and it is never more than 2% slower than the current code. The benchmark leaves out the work of the flush listener, so the gain in production is smaller in the steady state.

All changes to {{ListenerContext}} are under synchronized methods, so the {{AtomicReference}} is redundant.

Also, `setRebalanceListener(RebalanceListener)` is used by testing only, and the test doesn't need that since the `subscriptionState` is a mock object. We could use following code instead

 
{code:java}
        // Fatal error received in response for the last heartbeat. Member should remain in FATAL
        // state but no callbacks should be triggered because the member already left the group.
        mockListener(new CounterConsumerRebalanceListener());
