The {{TopologyTestDriver}} constructor sets up the global task and the stream task, which opens their state stores. If that setup throws, the constructor does not close what it has opened. The caller gets no driver object, so it cannot call {{close()}} either, and the stores stay open until the JVM exits.

For a persistent store this leaves the RocksDB instance open and its {{LOCK}} file held.

h3. When it happens

Any failure after the stores are opened, for example:
* the task is treated as corrupted during initialization ({{TaskCorruptedException}} under exactly-once),
* a processor throws from {{init()}}.

h3. How to reproduce
{code:java}
final Topology topology = new Topology();
topology.addSource("source", "input");
topology.addProcessor("processor", () -> new Processor<String, Long, Void, Void>() {
    @Override
    public void init(final ProcessorContext<Void, Void> context) {
        store = context.getStateStore("store");
        throw new IllegalStateException("failed to initialize");
    }

    @Override
    public void process(final Record<String, Long> record) { }
}, "source");
topology.addStateStore(
    Stores.keyValueStoreBuilder(Stores.persistentKeyValueStore("store"), Serdes.String(), Serdes.Long()),
    "processor");

assertThrows(StreamsException.class, () -> new TopologyTestDriverBuilder(topology).withConfig(config).build());

store.isOpen(); // true, expected false
{code}

The same happens for a global store whose processor fails to initialize.

It was found through {{SegmentedStoreWithoutSegmentsTest}} (KAFKA-21492), which expects a {{TaskCorruptedException}} from the constructor. After the exception, opening the segment directory again in the same JVM fails with "lock hold by current process", for each of the seven store types the test covers.

h3. Proposed fix

When the setup fails, the constructor closes the task dirty and closes the global state manager, then rethrows the original failure.

It does not clean the state directory as {{close()}} does. Under exactly-once the dirty close of the task deletes the task directory, as Kafka Streams does for a corrupted task.

